# /etc/dnscheck/zones   —   0644 root:root
#
# One row per zone. Five columns, whitespace separated:
#
#   <zone>  <signed|unsigned>  <warn-days|->  <label=ip,...|->  <ip,...|none|->
#
# "-" in any column means "use the default from dnscheck.env". Lists inside a
# column are separated by COMMAS, never spaces, so the columns stay columns —
# a space is what tells one column from the next, so a list containing one
# would silently become two columns. dnscheck.env accepts commas too, so the
# same list can be pasted either way round without editing.
# Anything after a '#' is a comment, and commenting out a row is how you stop
# checking a zone for an afternoon without losing what it said.
#
# This file is not a credential and does not need to be: zone names and
# authoritative addresses are public DNS data. HC_URL is the credential, and it
# stays in dnscheck.env, which is 0600 root:root and read by systemd as root.
# Internal nameserver addresses listed here are readable by any local account.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# state — signed or unsigned
#
# DECLARED, not detected. Auto-detecting "no DNSKEY, so it must be unsigned"
# would mean a zone whose signing STOPPED gets silently reclassified as working
# exactly as intended — the precise failure this tool exists to catch. Being
# signed is a fact about your intent, so you state it.
#
# While you are TURNING ON DNSSEC, leave the zone "unsigned" until the parent
# publishes your DS record. Between signing the zone and the registry adding
# the DS, every nameserver serves signatures and no resolver validates them —
# the zone is insecure, not broken, and it resolves fine for everyone. Marked
# "signed" it alarms for the days that takes; marked "unsigned" it is checked
# for reachability and agreement throughout, and the moment the DS appears the
# check tells you the zone now validates and should be moved. Flipping the
# column then is the deliberate act that starts watching its signatures.
#
# Get it wrong in either direction and the check says so: an unsigned zone
# marked signed alarms about RRSIGs that were never meant to exist, and a
# signed zone marked unsigned is reported the first time a validator returns AD
# for it, because otherwise its signature expiry would go unwatched.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# warn-days — alarm when a signature has this many days left
#
# Must be "-" for an unsigned zone, which has no signatures to expire. For a
# signed one, "-" takes WARN_DAYS from dnscheck.env; give a zone its own number
# when it is signed under a different policy. The threshold is measured against
# the floor of the RESIGNING cycle, not the signature's validity — see the
# WARN_DAYS notes in dnscheck.env.example, which explain why the default is 3
# and why 7 would alarm on a healthy zone a fifth of the time.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# nameservers — label=ip pairs, comma separated
#
# IPs, not hostnames: resolving a nameserver's name in order to find out
# whether DNS works is circular. The label is yours and appears in every alert
# line and every summary, so make it something you will recognise at 3am.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# validators — recursive resolvers, comma separated, or "none"
#
# These check the zone from OUTSIDE, which is the only way to catch a fault
# that is invisible from the nameservers themselves: a broken chain of trust,
# a DS record left behind at the parent after a zone was unsigned, or a
# delegation that has gone missing at the registry.
#
# Each validator is asked two questions. First for the zone's apex SOA, which
# proves it reached YOUR ZONE rather than an ancestor's opinion of it — ask any
# validating resolver about a name under a delegation that no longer exists and
# it returns NXDOMAIN with the AD flag set, because the PARENT's proof of
# nonexistence validates perfectly. Then for a random label that has never been
# queried, which forces a fresh resolution and exercises the NSEC/NSEC3 denial
# of existence, the half of DNSSEC that breaks quietly. A signed zone must
# validate on both.
#
# "none" for a zone no outside resolver can see — an internal zone on a private
# TLD, for instance. Say so explicitly, because the alternative is worse than
# it looks: a resolver asked about a name under a TLD that does not exist gets
# a signed proof from the ROOT that it does not, which validates — so the zone
# can look both resolvable and correctly signed while nothing about it has been
# verified at all.
#
# Naming an internal resolver here only helps if it actually SERVES the zone.
# A forwarder that does not hold it will pass the query upstream, and what
# comes back is the root's opinion, not your zone's. The apex-SOA question
# above is what tells those two apart.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Replace every row below. These are documentation values.

# zone                state     warn  nameservers                    validators
example.com           signed    -     -                              -
example.net           signed    5     -                              -
example.org           unsigned  -     -                              -

# An internal zone on its own servers, validated by its own resolver:
# internal.example     signed    -     lan1=10.0.0.53,lan2=10.0.0.54  10.0.0.53
#
# The same zone where there is no internal validating resolver to ask:
# internal.example     signed    -     lan1=10.0.0.53,lan2=10.0.0.54  none
